<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Dulkadirids</h1><p class="st-meta">1337 CE – 1522 CE · capital Elbistan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkmen beylik around Elbistan and Marash that served as the buffer, and the battleground, between the Mamluks and the Ottomans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1337 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluks</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayn-al-din-karaja">Zayn al-Din Karaja Bey</a> as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a> and the surrounding uplands of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a>, creating a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state on their northern frontier.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Dulkadirids led pastoral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes, raised horses and controlled the passes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> tried to control them through marriage and by backing rival claimants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-suwar">Shah Suwar</a> rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> with Ottoman encouragement and was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1472. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-bozkurt">Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt</a>, a long-reigning ruler, built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras">Marash</a> and resisted the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Ala al-Dawla failed to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I's</a> army defeated and killed him in 1515. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-bey-of-dulkadir">Ali Bey</a> ruled as an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> until he was executed in 1522 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> was annexed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dulkadirids.jpg" alt="Dulkadirids" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Lord Leatherface at Turkish Wikipedia — English translation by Morningstar1814 (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beylicats_d%25u2019Anatolie_vers_1330-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1337 CE – 1522 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Elbistan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karaja Bey, Shah Suwar, Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dulkadir Beylik, Dulkadiroğulları, Zulkadir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=dulkadirids" aria-label="Dulkadirids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5606 805 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dulkadirids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5926 1061L5815 1335M5926 1061L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5815 1335h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5926 1061h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5926 1061h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Elbistan. <a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=dulkadirids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
